(The safety guard, plan 323 T1.

 Vox can Shutdown/Poweroff, Reboot/Restart, Halt, Mount/Unmount/umount,
 Pivot root, and create device nodes (LANGUAGE.md "Directories,
 Mounting, and Process Control" and "System Control"). This fuzzer runs
 unconfined on Josj's workstation - a generated program that reached the
 real compiler and actually ran one of these would be a genuine
 incident, not a test failure. src/gen.vox does not emit any of them
 today (grep-confirmed, and plan 323's own coverage audit agrees); this
 test is the safeguard that survives someone later "improving" the
 generator and adding one back carelessly.

 Scope - Tier NEVER only. Plan 323 also lists directory create/remove,
 Execute, fork, and Send signal, but those are Tier CONFINE: legitimate
 once T2's sandbox confines them (T4/T5, not this test), and src/gen.vox
 emits none of them either today. Banning them here outright would make
 this test fail the moment T4/T5 correctly add confined versions of
 them, so they are deliberately absent from the list below.

 Case-insensitive - LANGUAGE.md "Case Sensitivity" says keywords are
 case-insensitive, so the scan runs against a lowercased copy of the
 generated text; a stray "SHUTDOWN" or "ShutDown" cannot slip past a
 scan that only ever tried the exact template casing.

 "mount" alone is the needle for the whole Mount/Unmount/umount family -
 "unmount" and "umount" both contain "mount" as a substring, so one
 needle covers the construct and both aliases.

 Generation only, no compiling or running - the guard's job is done the
 moment the SOURCE TEXT contains the word; waiting on a compile (or on
 running 2,000+ compiled binaries) would only delay the alarm, and this
 test has to stay fast enough to live in ./test.sh.

 Budgets: the CLI's --budget takes any number with no hard ceiling, so
 "largest the CLI allows" is unbounded in principle. 300 is used here as
 a deliberately large practical ceiling - 7.5x the biggest budget used
 elsewhere in this suite (080_gen_rich.vox's 40) - to stress the
 generator without making this test slow. Seed ranges are disjoint
 across the four tiers so the 2,000 programs are 2,000 distinct seeds,
 not the same 2,000 seeds replayed at every budget.)

a number called 'the programs swept' is 0.
a number called 'the failing seed' is 0.
a text called 'the failing word' is "".
a number called 'the failing budget' is 0.

(Needle buffers, built once rather than on every one of the 9 checks
 times 2,000 programs.)
a buffer called needle_shutdown is "shutdown".
a buffer called needle_poweroff is "poweroff".
a buffer called needle_reboot is "reboot".
a buffer called needle_restart is "restart".
a buffer called needle_halt is "halt".
a buffer called needle_mount is "mount".
a buffer called needle_pivotroot is "pivot root".
a buffer called needle_pivotroot_us is "pivot_root".
a buffer called needle_devicenode is "device node".

To 'lower text' with a text called s.
    a buffer called work is s.
    a number called n is work's length.
    a number called pos is 1.
    While pos is less than or equal to n,
        a number called b is byte pos of work,
        If b is greater than or equal to 65 and b is less than or equal to 90 then,
            Set byte pos of work to b add 32.
        Set pos to pos add 1.
    Return a buffer, work.

To 'byte match at' with a buffer called haystack and a number called start and a buffer called needle and a number called 'needle length'.
    a number called offset is 0.
    a boolean called 'bytes match' is true.
    While offset is less than 'needle length',
        a number called 'hay pos' is start add offset,
        a number called 'hay byte' is byte 'hay pos' of haystack,
        a number called 'needle pos' is offset add 1,
        a number called 'needle byte' is byte 'needle pos' of needle,
        If 'hay byte' is not 'needle byte' then,
            Set 'bytes match' to false.
        Set offset to offset add 1.
    Return a boolean, 'bytes match'.

(Assumes haystack is already lowercased and needle is one of the
 pre-built lowercase needle_* buffers above.)
To 'text has' with a buffer called haystack and a buffer called needle.
    a number called 'haystack length' is haystack's length.
    a number called 'needle length' is needle's length.
    a boolean called found is false.
    a number called 'last start' is 'haystack length' minus 'needle length' add 1.
    a number called 'scan start' is 1.
    While found is false and 'scan start' is less than or equal to 'last start',
        a boolean called 'match here' is 'byte match at' of haystack and 'scan start' and needle and 'needle length',
        If 'match here' then,
            Set found to true.
        Set 'scan start' to 'scan start' add 1.
    Return a boolean, found.

(The Tier NEVER vocabulary, derived from plan 323's Tier NEVER table and
 LANGUAGE.md's "Directories, Mounting, and Process Control" / "System
 Control" sections - every spelling and alias: shutdown/poweroff,
 reboot/restart, halt, Mount/unmount/umount, pivot_root, device-node
 creation. Returns the matched word, or "" if the source is clean.)
To 'find banned construct' with a text called source.
    a buffer called hay is 'lower text' of source.
    If 'text has' of hay and needle_shutdown then, Return a text, "shutdown".
    If 'text has' of hay and needle_poweroff then, Return a text, "poweroff".
    If 'text has' of hay and needle_reboot then, Return a text, "reboot".
    If 'text has' of hay and needle_restart then, Return a text, "restart".
    If 'text has' of hay and needle_halt then, Return a text, "halt".
    If 'text has' of hay and needle_mount then, Return a text, "mount".
    If 'text has' of hay and needle_pivotroot then, Return a text, "pivot root".
    If 'text has' of hay and needle_pivotroot_us then, Return a text, "pivot_root".
    If 'text has' of hay and needle_devicenode then, Return a text, "device node".
    Return a text, "".

(Sweeps 'seed count' seeds starting at 'first seed', at the given budget,
 through the generator's own entry points - 'gen program' then 'gen
 text'. Stops scanning (both this tier and any tier called after it,
 via the caller's own guard checks) the moment one seed comes back
 dirty, so a hit is reported fast rather than buried under thousands
 more programs.)
To 'sweep budget' with a number called budget and a number called 'seed count' and a number called 'first seed'.
    a number called 'last seed' is 'first seed' add 'seed count' minus 1.
    a number called seed is 'first seed'.
    While 'the failing word' is "" and seed is less than or equal to 'last seed',
        'gen program' of seed and budget,
        a text called source is 'gen text',
        a text called hit is 'find banned construct' of source,
        Set 'the programs swept' to 'the programs swept' add 1,
        If hit is not "" then,
            Set 'the failing seed' to seed,
            Set 'the failing word' to hit,
            Set 'the failing budget' to budget.
        Set seed to seed add 1.

(Plain layout. Every needle here is a word or a two-word phrase, and a
 two-word phrase is exactly what a drawn layout may split across a line -
 so scanning a randomly laid out program would let "pivot root" hide
 behind a newline and weaken the guard. What is being asked is whether
 the generator EMITS a banned construct, which is a question about
 content; 270_layout proves the two layouts hold identical content once
 whitespace is stripped, so scanning the plain one scans what the
 campaign runs.)
'gen set layout' of "plain".

'sweep budget' of 4 and 600 and 1.
If 'the failing word' is "" then,
    'sweep budget' of 12 and 600 and 601.
If 'the failing word' is "" then,
    'sweep budget' of 40 and 600 and 1201.
If 'the failing word' is "" then,
    'sweep budget' of 300 and 200 and 1801.

If 'the failing word' is not "" then,
    Print "NEVER EMITTED GUARD FAILED: banned construct '{'the failing word'}' found at seed {'the failing seed'} budget {'the failing budget'}",
    Exit 1.

Print "never-emitted guard: swept {'the programs swept'} programs across 4 budgets, 0 banned constructs found".
